<!--
    target-level is the Shipping FCM Version of the *vendor* implementation,
    not the system. Sony's stock SKU manifests (vendor/etc/vintf/
    manifest_parrot.xml and manifest_ravelin.xml, odm/etc/vintf/manifest_ds.xml
    and manifest_ss.xml) declare 6, for which Android 17 ships no framework
    matrix, so extract-files.py raises all four to 7 and this file matches.
    The vendor and the ODM manifest must agree: at runtime libvintf merges
    them and refuses two different levels ("Conflicting target-level"), which
    leaves no device manifest, so no vendor HAL can register. The build-time
    check cannot see that, because it runs with an empty sku.

    7 is also the highest level this vendor can claim: the 5.10 kernel has no
    FCM 8 requirements, FCM 8 drops HIDL HALs the stock blobs serve, and the
    framework serves netd@1.1, which netmgrd uses, only up to level 7. No
    <kernel> element: from level 7 on, assemble_vintf rejects an explicit
    kernel level, and libvintf infers it from target-level.

    THIS FILE IS NOT READ ON THE DEVICE while the vendor sku is set.
    vendor/etc/init/init.qti.qcv.rc sets ro.boot.product.vendor.sku=parrot at
    early-init and vendor.prop sets ro.boot.product.hardware.sku=ds, so
    libvintf loads Sony's vendor/etc/vintf/manifest_parrot.xml plus the
    fragments in vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/, merges
    odm/etc/vintf/manifest_ds.xml, and skips manifest.xml. Only with an unset
    vendor sku does it take this file instead, still merged with
    manifest_ds.xml. The build-time checkvintf reads it because it runs with
    an empty sku.

    A HAL declared only here is therefore undeclared at runtime, and
    hwservicemanager refuses its registration ("getTransport: Cannot find
    entry"). That kept fingerprint and atrace from working until 2026-09-11.
    Declare a HAL with a fragment instead. When a source module already owns
    the stock fragment's install path (kati: "overriding commands for
    target"), extract it under a -sony name, as proprietary-files.txt does for
    fingerprint and atrace. Do not declare the same instance here as well:
    checkvintf rejects the duplicate ("Conflicting FqInstance").
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device" target-level="7">
    <!-- No android.hardware.cas: the com.android.hardware.cas APEX serves the
         AIDL HAL (android.hardware.cas-service.example, running as
         vendor.cas-default). Sony's HIDL cas@1.2 blob has no .rc and never runs. -->

    <!-- No android.hardware.audio.core (AIDL): audio is the stock HIDL
         audio@7.0 HAL below. -->
    <!-- Stock HIDL audio HAL (vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.audio.service_64).
         frameworks/av/media/libaudiohal/FactoryHal.cpp falls back from AIDL to
         HIDL 7.1/7.0/6.0 and looks up exactly these two:
             HIDL devices -> android.hardware.audio        / IDevicesFactory
             HIDL effects -> android.hardware.audio.effect / IEffectsFactory
         At runtime both come from Sony's manifest_parrot.xml; these copies
         only serve the build-time checkvintf (see the note at the top). -->
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>7.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IDevicesFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio.effect</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>7.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IEffectsFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
</manifest>
